## Results and discussion
## Design and characterization of P1-P4 and Pdots
In the dual intramolecular RET mechanism, AIE-active TPE plays key roles (A1/D2). As the proof-of-concept, three model polymers (P2-P4) were designed (Fig. 1A). Specifically, P2 included the moieties of D1 and A1/D2 with single ECL-RET, while P3 consisted of A2 without ECL-RET. Besides, P4 was composed of more A1/D2 and less A2 compared to P1 and acted as the model of incompletely dual ECL-RET. In the P1, the optimial redio of A1/D2 to A2 was 8:2 (Fig. S1). The detailed synthetic routes of P1-P4 were modified according to our previous reports (Scheme S1).26-28 The Pdots could be prepared by a nanoprecipitation method utilizing polymer as luminescent precursor and poly(styrene-comaleic anhydride) (PSMA) as functional reagent.
P1 exhibited three distinct absorption peaks in THF solution centered at 300, 349 and $429{\mathsf{n m}}$ , respectively (Fig. 1B). The peaks at 300 and $349\ \mathsf{n m}$ also occurred in the absorption spectrum of
P2. Compared with P2 and P3, the shoulder absorption peak at $429{\mathsf{n m}}$ could be assigned to DMDO chromophore (A2), indicating the intramolecular charge transfer process from the electron-rich carbazole donor to the electron-deficient DMDO acceptor via TPE bridge linker.29 The identifiable overlap of the absorption of P1 dots with the emission of P2 dots in $H_{2}O$ demonstrated the feasibility of the second FRET process from TPE moiety (A1/D2) to DMDO (A2) (Fig. 1B).
The fluorescent spectrum of P1 in THF showed a weak emission situated at $412\mathsf{n m}$ with shoulder peak at $433~\mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 1C), which could be attributed to the carbazole moiety. Upon addition of $H_{2}O$ in THF, these peaks gradually decreased and disappeared until the water fraction $(f_{\mathrm{w}})$ reached $70\%$ . Meanwhile, the peak at $496\ \mathsf{n m}$ from the emission of TPE moiety gradually increased from $f_{\mathrm{w}}=0$ to $30\%$ , and then disappeared at $f_{\mathrm{w}}=40\%$ , indicating the first intramolecular FRET process from carbazole moiety to TPE moiety.18 With the increasing $f_{\mathrm{w}},$ a new emission peak assigned to DMDO chromophore appeared at $527\ \mathsf{n m}.$ which gradually redshifted to $548~\mathsf{n m}$ at $f_{\mathrm{w}}$ of $90\%$ with an abrupt increase of peak intensity at $80\%$ to $90\%$ , demonstrating the presence of the second intramolecular FRET process in P1 dots at high water fractions.
The P1 dots showed a fluorescence lifetime τ of 8.00 ns $\left(\mathsf{C H}|\mathsf{S}\mathsf{Q}=1.03\right)$ , which was much longer than 1.58 ns of P2 dots $\scriptstyle\left(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}|\mathsf{S}\mathsf{O}=1.00\right)$ (Fig. 1D), demonstrating the different fluorescent emission moieties and the presence of the second FRET in P1 dots. The fluorescence lifetime of 2.68 ns $\mathsf{\Lambda}^{\prime}\mathsf{C H I S O}\mathsf{=}1.09\$ for P3 dots was also slightly longer than that of P2 dots. Therefore, the fluorescence spectra of P1 and P3 dots exhibited different emission peak wavelengths from that of P2 dots (Fig. 1E). When the fraction of DMDO decreased to 0.05, a weak emission peak occured at $530~\mathsf{n m}$ , indicating the weakened FRET in P4 dots due to the incompletely dual RET, which also demonstrated the FRET effect from TPE to DMDO.
## Dual ECL-RET of P1 dots
The relative ECL efficiency of $50\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ P1 dots vs. 1 mM $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}2+$ was calculated to be $23.1\%$ (Fig. S4), which was obviously higher than those of commercial poly[2-methoxy-5-(2-ethyl hexyloxy)-1,4-(1-cyanovinylene-1,4-phenylene)] Pdots $(11.22\%),^{12}$ and the previously reported triple-component Pdots (Table S1). The ECL emission of P1 dots also showed the lowest onset and peak potentials, compared with several commercial Pdots (Fig. S5 and Table S2), demonstrating the distinguished performances of P1 dots in ECL emission.
To assess the intrinsic energy parameters and infer the relative stability of electron/hole injection P1 dots, annihilation ECL was performed in the absence of TprA. As expected, P1 dots could be electrochemically oxidized to form cation radicals or reduced to anion radicals in PBS by injecting holes or electrons into P1 dots (Fig. S6A),33 which led to a strong annihilation ECL emission with a peak at $+1.07\vee$ during anodic scanning, and a weak ECL signal in cathodic region with an onset potential of $-1.30{\sf V}_{.}$ , respectively (Fig. S6B). No matter in the oxidative or reductive process-initiated ECL transients, distinguished ECL only appeared at $+1.10\vee$ (Fig. S6C and S6D), demonstrating that the radical for anodic ECL emission was more stable.34 In addition, considering the same excited species in both annihilation and coreactant ECL emission, the ECL band gap of P1 dots could be estimated from the onset potentials of anodic and cathodic annihilation ECL emission at $+0.84$ and $-1.30\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ to be $2.14~\mathsf{e V}$ (Fig. S6B). It was consistent with the energy level (2.16 eV) from CV of P1 that gave quasi-reversible oxidation peaks with the onset potential of $+0.78\mathrm{~V~}$ resulting from the oxidation of TPE moiety35 and onset reduction potential of -1.38 V (Fig. S7).
## Target-mediated enzymatical circulation amplification
The designed P1 dots with high ECL efficiency showed very low cytotoxicity (Fig. S8), providing promising application in bioanalysis. Using two kinds of tumor-associated membrane proteins, MUC1 and HER2, as model targets, Apt1 for coding the targets by the specific recognition of aptamers to proteins, HP2 for performing target-mediated enzymatical circulation amplification, HP3 for capturing the released codes (S-DNA) on a DNA array, and capture DNA (cDNA) for labelling the ECL probe (P1 dots@PCN-224), the sensitive ECL imaging platform was developed. The detailed DNA sequences were listed in Table S3. As shown in Fig. 3A and 3B, the recognition of aptamers to proteins induced the hybridization of
Apt1 with HP2 (lines a-b), which produced a band at low migration, and Exonuclease III (Exo III) could digest the Apt1/HP2 duplex to produce the S-DNA with a band at high migration (line c), which hybridized with HP3 to form a new band (line d). The hybridization product of S-DNA and HP3 could be recognized by cDNA to form another band at relatively low migration (line e). These phenomena verified the feasibility of the designed imaging strategy, including coding, amplification, and capture processes. Moreover, the hybridization between HP3 and cDNA, as well as among Apt1, HP2 and HP3, in the absence of target could be excluded (lines f-g).
The specific recognition of Apt1 toward targets was further demonstrated with confocal laser scanning microscopic (CLSM) imaging on living cell surface (Fig. 3C and 3D). Compared to the treatment with Random 1-FAM and Random 2-FAM, the bright fluorescence was observed on both MUC1-over-expressed MCF-7 cells36 upon incubation with Apt1’-FAM and HER2-over-expressed SK-BR-3 cells37 upon incubation with Apt1’’-FAM, respectively.
## Characterization of P1 dots@PCN-224 probe
The transmission electron micrographs (TEM) images of P1 dots and metal-organic framework (MOF) nanoparticles (PCN-224) showed the spherical and monodisperse feature with the average diameter around $11\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 4A) and $83~\mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 4B), respectively. The ECL probe was prepared with PCN-224 as carrier to assemble the cDNA functionalized P1 dots through electrostatic adsorption (Scheme 1A), which could further enhance the ECL emission. Morphologic characteristics also demonstrated the successful assembly of the probe, which showed good distribution and dense loading of P1 dots on PCN-224 (Fig. 4C). Moreover, the P1 dots@PCN-224 probe could maintain stable hydrodynamic diameter (Fig. S9), which proved the good stablity of the probe. In addition, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data demonstrated the pure phase of PCN-224 (Fig. 4D), and the positively charged PCN-224 38 could easily adsorbe the negatively charged P1 dots-DNA (Fig. 4E). After loading P1 dotscDNA, the MOFs exhibited two new peaks at 247 and $358~\mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 4F), attributed to the merge of MOF absorption around $224~\mathsf{n m}$ with cDNA absorption at $255~\mathsf{n m}$ , and the absorption of P1 dots at $348{\mathsf{n m}}$ (Fig. 1B), respectively.
## Protein profiling and cell identification
The assembly process of DNA sequences on Au/ITO for ECL imaging was examined with electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (Figure S10). Upon the self-assembly of HP3 and then blocking with 6-mercapto-1-hexanol (MCH) on Au/indium tin oxide (ITO), the electron-transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ successively increased. After incubating with S-DNA, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ further increased, indicating the successful capture of the released S-DNA in target recycling mixture.
In order to achieve highly sensitive imaging, Apt1 concentration, digestion time, the amount of Exo III, HP3 concentration and incubation time of probe were optimized, sequentially (Fig. S11). Under the optimized detection conditions, the probe was introduced to DNA array for ECL imaging measurements through the hybridization of cDNA with opened HP3. With the increasing concentration of MUC1 and HER2, the ECL brightness increased correspondingly and linearly depended on the logarithm of protein concentration in the range of $1\mathsf{p g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ to $5~{\mathsf{n g}}~{\mathsf{m L}}^{-1}$ for MUC1 and 5 $\mathsf{p g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ to $10~\mathsf{n g}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ for HER2 with the limits of detection (LOD) 1 $\mathsf{p g}\mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ and $5~{\mathsf{p g}}~{\mathsf{m L}}^{-1}$ at a signal-to-noise ratio of 3, respectively (Fig. 5A). The sensitive strategy based on triple amplification processes showed more excellent performance comparing with several previous reports (Table S4). The ECL responses to different proteins such as epithelial cell adhesion molecule (EpCAM), carcinoebryonic antigen (CEA), alpha fetoprotein (AFP), neuron-specific enolase (NSE), MUC1 and HER2 demonstrated that the proposed imaging method exhibited high specificity to the target proteins (Fig. 5B).
The proposed strategy could be effectively extended to the quantitation of membrane proteins on variety types of cells. It is well known that HepG2 cells do not expressed MUC1, and NB-4 cells do not also overexpress MUC1 and HER2. Thus the ECL image showed obviously different responses to three kinds of breast cancer cells (MCF-7, MDA-MB-231 and SK-BR-3) (Fig. 5C). The average amounts of MUC1 on each MCF-7, MDA-MB-231 and SK-BR-3 cell
were determined to be 13.1, 7.64 and $3.71\ \times10^{6}$ sites, while the average amounts of HER2 on these cells were 6.1, 7.1 and $301\times10^{5}$ , respectively (Fig. 5D). These results were close to the data reported previously.39,40 The relative standard deviation for three parallel measurements was less than $12.2\%$ (Fig. S12), indicating the high specificity and good accuracy of the imaging strategy.
## Conclusions
A dual intramolecular RET mechanism is proposed with a newly designed triple-component P1 dots to greatly enhance the ECL emission of Pdots. The P1 dots contain two pairs of energy donors and energy acceptors, which reduces the path of energy transmission and energy loss, and thus show high ECL efficiency at relatively low ECL potential. Through loading DNA functionalized P1 dots on MOF as a carrier, a sensitive ECL probe has been structured for highly sensitive bioanalysis by the introduction of target-mediated enzymatic circulation amplification as the third amplification process. The proposed ECL imaging platform shows excellent performance for sensitive and specific quantification of cell surface MUC1 and HER2 with the LOD of pg mL-1 level, and can be used for screening of breast cancer cells. By using different aptamers to specifically code the targets, the designed method can be conveniently extended for simultaneous visual analysis of different targets. This dual intramolecular RET strategy provides a new concept on ECL enhancement, and broadens the application of Pdots in ECL imaging and bioanalysis.
